# <<PROJECT_NAME>> — FTC robot code

Team package: `<<PACKAGE>>`

## How to work in this repo

This project is built with the **ftc-mcp** server. Use its resources and tools.

### Architecture (see `ftc://guide/architecture`)
- **Subsystems** live in `subsystems/<Name>/` — `<Name>.java` (`implements Subsystem`) +
  `@Configurable <Name>Config.java` for all tunables. Hardware is passed into the
  constructor. Never talk to hardware outside a subsystem.
- **`core/Robot.java`** owns every subsystem + the Pedro `Follower`, holds the
  **action** state machines (one private `enum <Action>State` each), and exposes one
  method per action (`shoot()`, `score()`, …) plus `isXBusy()` helpers.
- **`core/vars/Action.java`** is the high-level action enum. **`SubsystemData`** is the
  per-loop shared bus between subsystems.
- **TeleOp**: `opmodes/teleop/BaseTeleOp` (abstract `OpMode`) + thin per-alliance
  subclasses. **Auto**: `opmodes/auto/base/BaseAuto` runs an `AutoRoutine`; routine
  subclasses build a step list with `move()/shootSequence()/turnTo()/waitUntil()`.
- **Never implement a drivetrain subsystem.** Driving goes through the Pedro `Follower`
  directly (`setTeleOpDrive`, `followPath`) — it is not a `Subsystem`.

### Accuracy (see `ftc://guide/accuracy`)
Only state what you can verify. The SDKs are poorly documented — confirm every method,
signature and constant against the decompiled reference (`sdk_search`, `sdk_class`,
`ftc://sdk/*`) or the codebase before using it. If you cannot verify something, say so
and go check — never present a guess as fact.

### Code style (see `ftc://guide/code-style`)
Organized, simple, efficient. Short one-line comments describing *what a method does /
why* — never walls of text, never restating the code. Any constant set from a guess
rather than a measured/verified value gets a `// TODO:` note on that line.

### Mandatory loop for every change (see `ftc://guide/workflow`, `ftc://guide/planning`)
1. Ask the user whatever is needed to understand the task.
2. For anything non-trivial: confirm your understanding + intended approach with the
   user, draft a plan, run **plan-pro-advocate** and **plan-critic**, reconcile, then
   present the reconciled plan to the user and get their go-ahead.
3. Implement with short comments.
4. Update `map/project-map.yaml` to reflect the change.
5. Run the `update_map` tool.
6. **Review the changed files yourself** — there's no Android/Gradle toolchain here, so
   don't try to compile; read the diff and check signatures, types, imports and
   architecture fit by hand (see `ftc://guide/workflow`).
7. Run the `log_change` tool with the prompt, your reply, and the files changed.

`map/MAP.md` must always fully describe the project's logic — a reader should understand
the whole codebase from the map alone.
